2,Wikipedia_Exp_14.04,Wikipedia_Exp_13.04,33,Female,sg_1776172391075_2kvlx8,diffuse,1,,psychology,high-psychology,What brain mechanisms help people make scientific discoveries?,search,2026-04-14T13:16:39.951Z,,,,,,brain mechanisms in ,,,,,,,,,,,
2,Wikipedia_Exp_14.04,Wikipedia_Exp_13.04,33,Female,sg_1776172391075_2kvlx8,diffuse,1,,psychology,high-psychology,What brain mechanisms help people make scientific discoveries?,paste,2026-04-14T13:17:54.753Z,,,,,,,,,,,"fMRI investigation found that unexpected findings were associated with particular brain activity. Unexpected findings were found to activate the prefrontal cortex as well as the left hemisphere in general. This suggests that unexpected findings provoke more attention, and the brain applies more linguistic, conscious systems to help explain those findings. This supports the idea that scientists are using particular abilities that exist to some extent in all humans",467,,,,,
2,Wikipedia_Exp_14.04,Wikipedia_Exp_13.04,33,Female,sg_1776172391075_2kvlx8,diffuse,1,,psychology,high-psychology,What brain mechanisms help people make scientific discoveries?,answer_snapshot,2026-04-14T13:17:56.756Z,,,,,,,"Psychologist Kevin Dunbar and colleagues estimate that between 30% and 50% of all scientific discoveries are accidental in some sense. fMRI investigation found that unexpected findings were associated with particular brain activity. Unexpected findings were found to activate the prefrontal cortex as well as the left hemisphere in general. This suggests that unexpected findings provoke more attention, and the brain applies more linguistic, conscious systems to help explain those findings. This supports the idea that scientists are using particular abilities that exist to some extent in all humans",602,,,,,,,debounce,,
2,Wikipedia_Exp_14.04,Wikipedia_Exp_13.04,33,Female,sg_1776172391075_2kvlx8,diffuse,1,,psychology,high-psychology,What brain mechanisms help people make scientific discoveries?,answer_snapshot,2026-04-14T13:18:10.288Z,,,,,,,"Psychologist Kevin Dunbar and colleagues estimate that between 30% and 50% of all scientific discoveries are accidental in some sense or are luck . fMRI investigation found that unexpected findings were associated with particular brain activity. Unexpected findings were found to activate the prefrontal cortex as well as the left hemisphere in general. This suggests that unexpected findings provoke more attention, and the brain applies more linguistic, conscious systems to help explain those findings. This supports the idea that scientists are using particular abilities that exist to some extent in all humans",615,,,,,,,debounce,,
2,Wikipedia_Exp_14.04,Wikipedia_Exp_13.04,33,Female,sg_1776172391075_2kvlx8,diffuse,1,,psychology,high-psychology,What brain mechanisms help people make scientific discoveries?,answer_snapshot,2026-04-14T13:18:18.878Z,,,,,,,"Psychologist Kevin Dunbar and colleagues estimate that between 30% and 50% of all scientific discoveries are accidental in some sense or are purely luck. fMRI investigation found that unexpected findings were associated with particular brain activity. Unexpected findings were found to activate the prefrontal cortex as well as the left hemisphere in general. This suggests that unexpected findings provoke more attention, and the brain applies more linguistic, conscious systems to help explain those findings. This supports the idea that scientists are using particular abilities that exist to some extent in all humans",621,,,,,,,debounce,,
