53,Wikipedia_Exp_14.04,Wikipedia_Exp_13.04,49,Female,sg_1776256452227_mshq8o,diffuse,1,,psychology,high-psychology,What brain mechanisms help people make scientific discoveries?,article_open,2026-04-15T12:49:21.054Z,Neuroscience,Neuroscience,,11159,link_click,,,,,,,,,,,,
53,Wikipedia_Exp_14.04,Wikipedia_Exp_13.04,49,Female,sg_1776256452227_mshq8o,diffuse,1,,psychology,high-psychology,What brain mechanisms help people make scientific discoveries?,paste,2026-04-15T12:50:33.571Z,,,,,,,,,,,"Cognitive neuroscience addresses the questions of how psychological functions are produced by neural circuitry. The emergence of powerful new measurement techniques such as neuroimaging (e.g., fMRI, PET, SPECT), EEG, MEG, electrophysiology, optogenetics and human genetic analysis combined with sophisticated experimental techniques from cognitive psychology allows neuroscientists and psychologists to address abstract questions such as how cognition and emotion are mapped to specific neural substrates. Although many studies hold a reductionist stance looking for the neurobiological basis of cognitive phenomena, recent research shows that there is an interplay between neuroscientific findings and conceptual research, soliciting and integrating both perspectives. For example, neuroscience research on empathy solicited an interdisciplinary debate involving philosophy, psychology and psychopathology.[62] Moreover, the neuroscientific identification of multiple memory systems related to different brain areas has challenged the idea of memory as a literal reproduction of the past, supporting a view of memory as a generative, constructive and dynamic process.[",1169,,,,,
53,Wikipedia_Exp_14.04,Wikipedia_Exp_13.04,49,Female,sg_1776256452227_mshq8o,diffuse,1,,psychology,high-psychology,What brain mechanisms help people make scientific discoveries?,answer_snapshot,2026-04-15T12:50:35.587Z,,,,,,,"Cognitive neuroscience addresses the questions of how psychological functions are produced by neural circuitry. The emergence of powerful new measurement techniques such as neuroimaging (e.g., fMRI, PET, SPECT), EEG, MEG, electrophysiology, optogenetics and human genetic analysis combined with sophisticated experimental techniques from cognitive psychology allows neuroscientists and psychologists to address abstract questions such as how cognition and emotion are mapped to specific neural substrates. Although many studies hold a reductionist stance looking for the neurobiological basis of cognitive phenomena, recent research shows that there is an interplay between neuroscientific findings and conceptual research, soliciting and integrating both perspectives. For example, neuroscience research on empathy solicited an interdisciplinary debate involving philosophy, psychology and psychopathology.[62] Moreover, the neuroscientific identification of multiple memory systems related to different brain areas has challenged the idea of memory as a literal reproduction of the past, supporting a view of memory as a generative, constructive and dynamic process.[",1169,,,,,,,debounce,,
53,Wikipedia_Exp_14.04,Wikipedia_Exp_13.04,49,Female,sg_1776256452227_mshq8o,diffuse,1,,psychology,high-psychology,What brain mechanisms help people make scientific discoveries?,answer_snapshot,2026-04-15T12:50:58.479Z,,,,,,,"Cognitive neuroscience addresses the questions of how psychological functions are produced by neural circuitry. The emergence of powerful new measurement techniques such as neuroimaging (e.g., fMRI, PET, SPECT), EEG, MEG, electrophysiology, optogenetics and human genetic analysis combined with sophisticated experimental techniques from cognitive psychology allows neuroscientists and psychologists to address abstract questions such as how cognition and emotion are mapped to specific neural substrates. Although many studies hold a reductionist stance looking for the neurobiological basis of cognitive phenomena, recent research shows that there is an interplay between neuroscientific findings and conceptual research, soliciting and integrating both perspectives. For example, neuroscience research on empathy solicited an interdisciplinary debate involving philosophy, psychology and psychopathology. Moreover, the neuroscientific identification of multiple memory systems related to different brain areas has challenged the idea of memory as a literal reproduction of the past, supporting a view of memory as a generative, constructive and dynamic process.[",1165,,,,,,,debounce,,
