59,Wikipedia_Exp_14.04,Wikipedia_Exp_13.04,21,Male,sg_1776257622048_utieoi,clumpy,2,,psychology,high-psychology,What brain mechanisms help people make scientific discoveries?,answer_snapshot,2026-04-15T13:19:52.612Z,,,,,,,"Scientific discoveries are innovative for society; just for example, in 3000 BC, Units of measurement were developed in the Americas as well as the major Bronze Age civilizations: Egypt, Mesopotamia, Elam and the Indus Valley. Another example would be Pythagoras, who is credited with mathematical and scientific discoveries, such as the Pythagorean theorem, Pythagorean tuning, the five regular solids, the theory of proportions, the sphericity of the Earth, the identity of the morning and evening stars as the planet Venus, and the division of the globe into five climatic zones. All of this can be credited to ",614,,,,,,,debounce,,
59,Wikipedia_Exp_14.04,Wikipedia_Exp_13.04,21,Male,sg_1776257622048_utieoi,clumpy,2,,psychology,high-psychology,What brain mechanisms help people make scientific discoveries?,answer_snapshot_cursor_leave,2026-04-15T13:19:53.862Z,,,,,,,"Scientific discoveries are innovative for society; just for example, in 3000 BC, Units of measurement were developed in the Americas as well as the major Bronze Age civilizations: Egypt, Mesopotamia, Elam and the Indus Valley. Another example would be Pythagoras, who is credited with mathematical and scientific discoveries, such as the Pythagorean theorem, Pythagorean tuning, the five regular solids, the theory of proportions, the sphericity of the Earth, the identity of the morning and evening stars as the planet Venus, and the division of the globe into five climatic zones. All of this can be credited to ",614,,,,,,,cursor_leave,,
59,Wikipedia_Exp_14.04,Wikipedia_Exp_13.04,21,Male,sg_1776257622048_utieoi,clumpy,2,,psychology,high-psychology,What brain mechanisms help people make scientific discoveries?,paste,2026-04-15T13:20:06.362Z,,,,,,,,,,,"Self-knowledge is a term in psychology, describing the information needed for an individual to answer the questions ""Who am I?"" and ""What am I like?"". ",151,,,,,
59,Wikipedia_Exp_14.04,Wikipedia_Exp_13.04,21,Male,sg_1776257622048_utieoi,clumpy,2,,psychology,high-psychology,What brain mechanisms help people make scientific discoveries?,answer_snapshot,2026-04-15T13:20:09.553Z,,,,,,,"Scientific discoveries are innovative for society; just for example, in 3000 BC, Units of measurement were developed in the Americas as well as the major Bronze Age civilizations: Egypt, Mesopotamia, Elam and the Indus Valley. Another example would be Pythagoras, who is credited with mathematical and scientific discoveries, such as the Pythagorean theorem, Pythagorean tuning, the five regular solids, the theory of proportions, the sphericity of the Earth, the identity of the morning and evening stars as the planet Venus, and the division of the globe into five climatic zones. All of this can be credited to self awareness within the brain. Self-knowledge is a term in psychology, describing the information needed for an individual to answer the questions ""Who am I?"" and ""What am I like?"".",797,,,,,,,debounce,,
59,Wikipedia_Exp_14.04,Wikipedia_Exp_13.04,21,Male,sg_1776257622048_utieoi,clumpy,2,,psychology,high-psychology,What brain mechanisms help people make scientific discoveries?,answer_snapshot_cursor_leave,2026-04-15T13:20:09.849Z,,,,,,,"Scientific discoveries are innovative for society; just for example, in 3000 BC, Units of measurement were developed in the Americas as well as the major Bronze Age civilizations: Egypt, Mesopotamia, Elam and the Indus Valley. Another example would be Pythagoras, who is credited with mathematical and scientific discoveries, such as the Pythagorean theorem, Pythagorean tuning, the five regular solids, the theory of proportions, the sphericity of the Earth, the identity of the morning and evening stars as the planet Venus, and the division of the globe into five climatic zones. All of this can be credited to self awareness within the brain. Self-knowledge is a term in psychology, describing the information needed for an individual to answer the questions ""Who am I?"" and ""What am I like?"".",797,,,,,,,cursor_leave,,
59,Wikipedia_Exp_14.04,Wikipedia_Exp_13.04,21,Male,sg_1776257622048_utieoi,clumpy,2,,psychology,high-psychology,What brain mechanisms help people make scientific discoveries?,paste,2026-04-15T13:20:26.265Z,,,,,,,,,,,"Self-knowledge requires both self-awareness and self-consciousness (aware of the fact that one is self-aware). While young infants and chimpanzees display some of the traits of self-awareness,[1] agency, and contingency;[2] they are not considered to be self-conscious. At some greater level of cognition, however, a self-conscious component emerges in addition to an increased self-awareness component, and then it becomes possible to ask ""What am I like?"", and to answer with self-knowledge, though self-knowledge has limits, as introspection has been said to be limited and complex, such as the consciousness of being conscious of oneself. [3]",646,,,,,
59,Wikipedia_Exp_14.04,Wikipedia_Exp_13.04,21,Male,sg_1776257622048_utieoi,clumpy,2,,psychology,high-psychology,What brain mechanisms help people make scientific discoveries?,answer_snapshot,2026-04-15T13:20:28.286Z,,,,,,,"Scientific discoveries are innovative for society; just for example, in 3000 BC, Units of measurement were developed in the Americas as well as the major Bronze Age civilizations: Egypt, Mesopotamia, Elam and the Indus Valley. Another example would be Pythagoras, who is credited with mathematical and scientific discoveries, such as the Pythagorean theorem, Pythagorean tuning, the five regular solids, the theory of proportions, the sphericity of the Earth, the identity of the morning and evening stars as the planet Venus, and the division of the globe into five climatic zones. All of this can be credited to self awareness within the brain. Self-knowledge is a term in psychology, describing the information needed for an individual to answer the questions ""Who am I?"" and ""What am I like?"". Self-knowledge requires both self-awareness and self-consciousness (aware of the fact that one is self-aware). While young infants and chimpanzees display some of the traits of self-awareness,[1] agency, and contingency;[2] they are not considered to be self-conscious. At some greater level of cognition, however, a self-conscious component emerges in addition to an increased self-awareness component, and then it becomes possible to ask ""What am I like?"", and to answer with self-knowledge, though self-knowledge has limits, as introspection has been said to be limited and complex, such as the consciousness of being conscious of oneself. [3]",1444,,,,,,,debounce,,
59,Wikipedia_Exp_14.04,Wikipedia_Exp_13.04,21,Male,sg_1776257622048_utieoi,clumpy,2,,psychology,high-psychology,What brain mechanisms help people make scientific discoveries?,answer_snapshot,2026-04-15T13:20:35.415Z,,,,,,,"Scientific discoveries are innovative for society; just for example, in 3000 BC, Units of measurement were developed in the Americas as well as the major Bronze Age civilizations: Egypt, Mesopotamia, Elam and the Indus Valley. Another example would be Pythagoras, who is credited with mathematical and scientific discoveries, such as the Pythagorean theorem, Pythagorean tuning, the five regular solids, the theory of proportions, the sphericity of the Earth, the identity of the morning and evening stars as the planet Venus, and the division of the globe into five climatic zones. All of this can be credited to self awareness within the brain. Self-knowledge is a term in psychology, describing the information needed for an individual to answer the questions ""Who am I?"" and ""What am I like?"". Self-knowledge requires both self-awareness and self-consciousness (aware of the fact that one is self-aware). While young infants and chimpanzees display some of the traits of self-awareness, agency, and contingency; they are not considered to be self-conscious. At some greater level of cognition, however, a self-conscious component emerges in addition to an increased self-awareness component, and then it becomes possible to ask ""What am I like?"", and to answer with self-knowledge, though self-knowledge has limits, as introspection has been said to be limited and complex, such as the consciousness of being conscious of oneself. [3]",1438,,,,,,,debounce,,
59,Wikipedia_Exp_14.04,Wikipedia_Exp_13.04,21,Male,sg_1776257622048_utieoi,clumpy,2,,psychology,high-psychology,What brain mechanisms help people make scientific discoveries?,answer_snapshot,2026-04-15T13:20:44.339Z,,,,,,,"Scientific discoveries are innovative for society; just for example, in 3000 BC, Units of measurement were developed in the Americas as well as the major Bronze Age civilizations: Egypt, Mesopotamia, Elam and the Indus Valley. Another example would be Pythagoras, who is credited with mathematical and scientific discoveries, such as the Pythagorean theorem, Pythagorean tuning, the five regular solids, the theory of proportions, the sphericity of the Earth, the identity of the morning and evening stars as the planet Venus, and the division of the globe into five climatic zones. All of this can be credited to self awareness within the brain. Self-knowledge is a term in psychology, describing the information needed for an individual to answer the questions ""Who am I?"" and ""What am I like?"". Self-knowledge requires both self-awareness and self-consciousness (aware of the fact that one is self-aware). While young infants and chimpanzees display some of the traits of self-awareness, agency, and contingency; they are not considered to be self-conscious. At some greater level of cognition, however, a self-conscious component emerges in addition to an increased self-awareness component, and then it becomes possible to ask ""What am I like?"", and to answer with self-knowledge, though self-knowledge has limits, as introspection has been said to be limited and complex, such as the consciousness of being conscious of oneself.",1434,,,,,,,debounce,,
59,Wikipedia_Exp_14.04,Wikipedia_Exp_13.04,21,Male,sg_1776257622048_utieoi,clumpy,2,,psychology,high-psychology,What brain mechanisms help people make scientific discoveries?,answer_snapshot,2026-04-15T13:21:19.436Z,,,,,,,"Scientific discoveries are innovative for society; just for example, in 3000 BC, Units of measurement were developed in the Americas as well as the major Bronze Age civilizations: Egypt, Mesopotamia, Elam and the Indus Valley. Another example would be Pythagoras, who is credited with mathematical and scientific discoveries, such as the Pythagorean theorem, Pythagorean tuning, the five regular solids, the theory of proportions, the sphericity of the Earth, the identity of the morning and evening stars as the planet Venus, and the division of the globe into five climatic zones. All of this can be credited to self awareness within the brain. Self-knowledge is a term in psychology, describing the information needed for an individual to answer the questions ""Who am I?"" and ""What am I like?"". Self-knowledge requires both self-awareness and self-consciousness (aware of the fact that one is self-aware). While young infants and chimpanzees display some of the traits of self-awareness, agency, and contingency; they are not considered to be self-conscious. At some greater level of cognition, however, a self-conscious component emerges in addition to an increased self-awareness component, and then it becomes possible to ask ""What am I like?"", and to answer with self-knowledge, though self-knowledge has limits, as introspection has been said to be limited and complex, such as the consciousness of being conscious of oneself. This is something that has a large effect on our ability to learn and understand new ideas and concepts that have not come to fruition before.",1577,,,,,,,debounce,,
